ask(1558,'keyword-intent','Which retained strings match the lecture’s two keyword-filter examples?', ['pick toys; clean room','pick up all toys; can clean this room','could please; can this','toys all; room can'],['The source’s examples retain pick/toys and clean/room.','These retain words that the source example filters out.','These retain filler rather than the intended content keywords.','These mixes do not match the illustrated outputs.'],kind='algorithm_trace',steps=['Read the first command and its shown retained action/object words.','Read the second command and its shown retained action/object words.','Report pick toys and clean room in source order.'],cites=[(1558,'statement','Two quoted command-to-keyword examples'),(1558,'figure','Crossed-out words leaving pick and toys')])
ask(1558,'keyword-intent','A filter retains only clean and room. It maps both “Clean the room” and “Do not clean the room” to [clean, room]. What failure does this prove?', ['The filter has discarded command polarity, so identical retained tokens need not mean identical intent','The two original commands necessarily requested the same action because their object matches','The second command is safely distinguishable from the retained tokens alone','The problem is only output pronunciation, not intent information'],['The negation distinguishes the requests but is absent after filtering.','The original commands have opposite action polarity.','The stipulated representations are identical and contain no polarity cue.','The information loss occurs before response speech.'],kind='algorithm_trace',ass=['The filter keeps only those two content words; no other state or metadata is retained.'],steps=['Filter the affirmative command to clean, room.','Filter the negative command to the same pair.','Observe that opposite intended polarity has been collapsed.'],conflict=KEY)
ask(1558,'keyword-intent','Which change directly addresses the polarity failure of a clean/toys-only intent filter?', ['Preserve negation and relevant argument relations when mapping the command','Make the TTS voice clearer while leaving the filtered representation unchanged','Always choose the highest keyword count even when a negative marker was discarded','Rename CLEAN_ROOM to CLEAN_TOYS without changing parsing behavior'],['Keeping semantic distinctions relevant to the task prevents this particular collision.','A clearer spoken answer cannot restore information lost before intent selection.','More retained content words do not repair discarded polarity.','Changing a label alone does not change what information the parser preserves.'],kind='scenario',conflict=KEY)
# Direct speech models and affective evidence.
ask(1559,'speechlm-motivation','Which pairing separates three different cascade limitations?', ['Lost tone: information bottleneck; waiting for sequential modules: latency; wrong transcript influencing reply: error propagation','Lost tone: latency; waiting for modules: error propagation; wrong transcript: information-preserving compression','Lost tone: model retraining; waiting for modules: vocabulary mismatch; wrong transcript: TTS-only distortion','Lost tone: intent grounding; waiting for modules: hidden state; wrong transcript: guaranteed downstream correction'],['These correspond to distinct information, timing and cascading-error mechanisms.','The categories are misassigned and a wrong transcript is not inherently information-preserving.','These are not the mechanisms identified by the examples.','The last claim incorrectly assumes downstream correction is guaranteed.'],kind='match')
ask(1559,'speechlm-motivation','Two recordings contain the identical words but different tone. A downstream system receives only the identical transcript and no acoustic metadata. What follows?', ['It cannot distinguish these recordings using acoustic differences that were not supplied','It can exactly recover both original pitch contours from their shared text alone','The transcripts must differ because every tone difference changes spelling','The TTS engine necessarily recreates the speaker’s original emotion'],['The stipulated text-only representation has discarded the distinguishing acoustic evidence.','Many waveforms can yield the same transcript.','Tone variation need not change word spelling.','Generated output tone is not necessarily recovery of the original speaker state.'],kind='scenario',ass=['No speaker metadata, audio features or contextual differences are available.'])
ask(1559,'speechlm-motivation','In a toy cascade, ASR is correct with probability 0.9; given correct ASR, intent interpretation is correct with probability 0.8; given both, response rendering is correct with probability 0.95. If success requires all three, what is its probability?', ['0.684','0.8833 approximately','0.900','0.650'],['The conditional chain gives 0.9×0.8×0.95=0.684.','That averages stage probabilities rather than computing joint success.','This ignores failures after recognition.','This subtracts the separate error probabilities 0.1+0.2+0.05 from one instead of using the supplied conditional chain.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',ass=['The latter two numbers are explicitly conditional on preceding success; success requires all three events. Independence is not additionally assumed.'],steps=['Let A be correct ASR, I correct intent and R correct rendering.','Use P(A∩I∩R)=P(A)P(I|A)P(R|A∩I).','Multiply 0.9×0.8×0.95 to obtain 0.684.'],formulas=[fm('P(A∩I∩R)=P(A)P(I|A)P(R|A∩I)',{'A':'Correct ASR event','I':'Correct interpretation event','R':'Correct rendering event','P':'Probability measure'},{'A,I,R':'events','P':'dimensionless'},['Supplied conditional probabilities; success is their intersection.'])],cites=[(1559,'statement','Cumulative-error bullet')])
ask(1559,'speechlm-motivation','What is the central change offered by the direct-speech approach described here?', ['Speech representations can be modeled without a mandatory intermediate transcript bottleneck','Every system must remove all optional text inputs and outputs','Every ASR error disappears because the model has fewer named boxes','No trained representation is needed because audio is already numerical'],['The architectural change avoids requiring plain text as the only intermediate representation.','The next slide’s diagram explicitly includes text paths.','End-to-end processing does not guarantee error-free output.','The model still needs suitable learned or engineered representations.'],cites=[(1559,'statement','SpeechLM motivation paragraph'),(1560,'figure','Optional text path alongside speech path')],primary=[SPEECH])
ask(1560,'speechlm-architecture','Which mapping matches the three main SpeechLM components?', ['Tokenizer: waveform to representation; LM: next-token modeling; vocoder: representation to waveform','Tokenizer: representation to waveform; LM: input recording; vocoder: text intent selection','Tokenizer: next-token modeling; LM: waveform playback; vocoder: input pattern matching','Tokenizer: output loudness control only; LM: fixed response-bank lookup only; vocoder: hidden state assignment'],['The components encode, model and synthesize speech respectively.','This exchanges input encoding with synthesis and assigns the LM a recording role.','The stages do not perform those functions in the shown architecture.','Those narrower unrelated operations do not match the component definitions.'],kind='match',cites=[(1560,'statement','Speech Tokenizer, Language Model and Vocoder definitions')])
ask(1560,'speechlm-architecture','The figure includes speech and text paths. Which reading is faithful to it?', ['The LM can participate in speech/text combinations; transcript captions do not prove mandatory internal ASR on every path','Every arrow is a separate mandatory ASR stage before the LM','The text path is the vocoder’s acoustic output, not a language sequence','The presence of a text caption proves the speech tokenizer outputs only words'],['The diagram includes cross-modal routing and descriptive transcript captions.','The paths are not all serial mandatory recognition stages.','The lower text output is distinct from the audio waveform above it.','A caption explaining audio content is not proof of an internal word-only representation.'],kind='diagram',cites=[(1560,'figure','Speech and text input/output rows, cross-modal arrows and transcript captions')])
ask(1560,'speechlm-architecture','What distinction is intended by discrete versus continuous speech representations?', ['Discrete tokens are quantized choices; continuous representations use real-valued features','Discrete tokens must be written English words; continuous features must be raw text','Discrete and continuous describe speaker gender rather than numerical representation','Continuous representations cannot be processed by neural models'],['The distinction concerns the representation domain, not whether the content is linguistic.','Discrete acoustic units need not be text words, and continuous features are not raw text.','The terms describe numerical encoding, not speaker identity.','Neural models routinely operate on real-valued representations.'],primary=[SPEECH])
ask(1560,'speechlm-architecture','In the depicted example, “The quick brown fox jumps over” is followed by “the lazy dog.” What does this illustrate?', ['Conditioned continuation that can be represented as speech or text','A required exact-response rule for every input containing fox','The vocoder independently choosing the sentence meaning without the LM','The tokenizer synthesizing the final waveform before any modeling'],['The diagram shows an input prefix and a generated continuation along language/speech paths.','The caption is an example, not a universal chatbot rule.','The LM is the modeled continuation stage.','The tokenizer is the encoding stage; waveform synthesis occurs downstream.'],kind='diagram',cites=[(1560,'figure','Input-prefix and output-continuation captions')])
ask(1561,'speechlm-tradeoffs','Which trade-off is described for semantic and acoustic tokens?', ['Semantic tokens emphasize content; acoustic tokens emphasize reconstruction detail; mixed designs seek both','Semantic tokens preserve every waveform detail; acoustic tokens contain only dictionary meanings','Semantic tokens are response templates; acoustic tokens are the robot’s intent labels','Using both types guarantees perfect meaning and zero latency'],['The lecture contrasts content representation with acoustic fidelity and motivates mixed approaches.','This reverses the stated emphasis.','These are speech representations, not those rule-system labels.','Balancing objectives does not eliminate all error or compute cost.'],primary=[SPEECH],cites=[(1561,'statement','Token Trade-Off bullet and mixed-token sub-bullet')])
ask(1561,'speechlm-tradeoffs','Which behavior is genuinely full-duplex rather than merely fast half-duplex?', ['Processing new user speech while generating the robot’s speech, with interruption handling','Generating a very fast reply only after the user is silent, while input processing stays disabled during output','Alternating short input and output turns with no simultaneous processing','Returning only a transcript instead of speech while the microphone is disabled'],['Full duplex concerns simultaneous input/output interaction, not just shorter turn latency.','That remains turn-taking without concurrent processing.','Shorter alternating turns are not by themselves full duplex.','This does not provide simultaneous spoken input/output.'],conflict=DUPLEX,cites=[(1561,'statement','Full-Duplex Interaction bullet')])
ask(1561,'speechlm-tradeoffs','A model accepts acoustic tokens. What further fact would support a claim that it handles conversational interruption?', ['Its architecture and tests show concurrent input handling and response cancellation or redirection','Its token vocabulary is larger than that of a text-only model','It can synthesize one expressive sentence when no user speaks','Its output sample rate is higher than the microphone sample rate'],['Interruption handling is a behavior and scheduling capability that needs direct support.','Vocabulary size alone does not establish concurrent input handling.','Single-turn expressive synthesis does not test interruption.','A sample-rate relationship does not establish dialogue control.'],kind='scenario',conflict=DUPLEX,cites=[(1561,'statement','Full-duplex listening while speaking and human interruptions')])
ask(1561,'speechlm-tradeoffs','Why does preserving a user’s tone not guarantee correct interpretation of the user’s intent?', ['Tone is an additional uncertain cue that must be interpreted with content and context','Tone and intent are the same measurable variable by definition','A faithful waveform reconstruction proves every spoken request is sincere','An acoustic token directly certifies permission for a physical action'],['Additional evidence can help without uniquely determining intent.','Tone and intended task are distinct concepts.','Faithful signal reproduction is not a sincerity test.','Representation of speech is not authorization or action validation.'],kind='scenario',conflict=DUPLEX,cites=[(1561,'statement','Preserving Emotion and accurately interpret user intent wording')])
ask(1562,'affective-cues','Which distinction captures the slide’s contrast between what was said and how it was said?', ['Word content versus features such as pitch, rhythm and intonation','Text character encoding versus the font used to display it','Intent labels versus the file extension of the transcript','A stored response index versus the number of database records'],['The contrast concerns linguistic content and vocal expression.','Font/encoding are not the cited vocal cues.','File extensions do not express the cited manner of speaking.','Database sizes are not acoustic expression features.'])
ask(1562,'affective-cues','If feelings are internal states, what is the justified limit on a robot’s emotion estimate?', ['External cues can support an uncertain estimate, not direct access to the person’s inner state','Internal states cannot be inferred at all, even probabilistically from relevant observations','A facial or vocal label is an infallible measurement of the hidden state','Storing earlier turns converts the estimate into direct mind reading'],['Hidden-state inference may use evidence while retaining uncertainty.','Inaccessibility to direct measurement is not impossibility of inference.','Observable expressions and model labels can be ambiguous.','Memory adds context, not direct access to private experience.'],conflict=AFFECT,cites=[(1562,'statement','Problem of Internal States bullet'),(1563,'statement','Third-person observer and pattern-recognition explanation')])
ask(1562,'affective-cues','A robot changes a dialogue-state variable from neutral to reassuring after a cue. What does this demonstrate by itself?', ['An implemented adaptive response policy, not evidence of subjective feeling','A biological need for reassurance in the robot','A guarantee that the user’s emotional state was correctly identified','A permanent change to every neural weight in the robot'],['Programmed or learned response behavior is distinct from establishing inner experience.','A software-state update does not demonstrate a biological need.','The cue may be ambiguous or misclassified.','A state-variable update is not necessarily model training.'],kind='scenario',ass=['Only the state change and resulting response behavior are observed.'],cites=[(1562,'statement','Need for Artificial Personality and simulated dynamic responses')])
ask(1563,'emotion-observer','Which inputs fit the lecture’s third-person pattern-recognition approach?', ['Observable facial motion and speech patterns','A direct readout of another person’s private thoughts','Only the robot’s own battery voltage, with no human cues','A guaranteed emotion label supplied by every transcript word'],['These are external modalities explicitly named by the source.','The robot is described as an external observer, not a mind reader.','The described human-emotion task needs evidence about the human.','Words do not each provide guaranteed emotion labels.'],kind='definition')
ask(1563,'emotion-observer','Facial cues suggest one interpretation while speech and context suggest another. What is a justified response of an emotion-aware interface?', ['Retain uncertainty and seek context or clarification rather than treating one cue as conclusive','Treat the facial label as ground truth regardless of the other observations','Discard all contextual information because emotion is always determined by one acoustic feature','Treat disagreement as proof that the person is deliberately deceptive'],['Conflicting observations weaken a categorical inference and motivate cautious interaction.','A predicted facial label is not direct access to the hidden state.','Multiple cues and context are part of the pattern-recognition problem.','Disagreement has many causes and does not prove deception.'],kind='scenario',ass=['No modality is stipulated to be infallible; the task is conversational adaptation, not diagnosis.'],conflict=EMOTION)
ask(1563,'emotion-observer','Which design best combines the lecture’s language and emotion lessons for a robot receiving an ambiguous spoken request?', ['Use content and available contextual/acoustic cues, clarify material ambiguity, then validate the selected action','Treat a confident emotion label as sufficient to determine both the requested object and a safe trajectory','Use only a keyword count and discard polarity, reference and tone because the words are enough','Execute the broadest matching routine while asking a clarifying question after motion begins'],['The combined pipeline distinguishes evidence, interpretation and validated physical behavior.','Emotion classification does not determine all task and geometric details.','The lecture’s limitations show why relevant information should not be discarded indiscriminately.','Clarification after an overbroad action does not resolve the ambiguity before execution.'],kind='synthesis',difficulty='medium',ass=['The request has unresolved material task scope; no motion plan has yet been validated.'],steps=['Preserve relevant content and contextual cues.','Resolve the material ambiguity before selecting task scope.','Validate the resulting action before physical execution.'],cites=[(1563,'statement','Observable multimodal cues'),(1558,'statement','Keyword-to-intent mapping'),(1556,'figure','Intent, state-machine and physical-action branch')],conflict=EMOTION)
